  "text_md": "24 M.H. SHAHZAMANIAN\n\nTheorem 4.2. We have θLCn(X) ≠ 0 if and only if n < 8. Proof. By Lemma 3.7, we have\n\nL̃a1a5 = {a1a5,a1a4a5,ana1a5,ana1a4a5} and\n\nR̃a1a5 = {a1a5,a1a2a5,a1a5a6,a1a2a5a6}.\n\nFor n ≥ 8, we have L ̃a1a5 = R ̃a1a5 = 4. By Lemma 5.8 in [12], we compute the submatrix L̃a1a5 ×R̃a1a5, extracted from the Cayley table (S,∗),\n\n- as follows: a1a5 a1a2a5 a1a5a6 a1a2a5a6\n\n![](<2503.06329_pg24_images/imageFile1.png>)\n\n![](<2503.06329_pg24_images/imageFile2.png>)\n\na1a5 a1a5 a1a2a5 a1a5a6 a1a2a5a6\n\n![](<2503.06329_pg24_images/imageFile3.png>)\n\na1a4a5 a1a4a5 ⋅ ⋅ ⋅ a5ana1 a5ana1 ⋅ ana1a5a6 ⋅\n\n![](<2503.06329_pg24_images/imageFile4.png>)\n\n![](<2503.06329_pg24_images/imageFile5.png>)\n\na4a5ana1 a4a5ana1 ⋅ ⋅ ⋅ where the zero is represented with a dot. To compute the entries the table,\n\n![](<2503.06329_pg24_images/imageFile6.png>)\n\n- at position (s,t), we only need to verify that (st)+ = s+ and (st)∗ = t∗.\n\n\nSince its determinant is zero, this is suﬃcient to show that the determinant of θLCn(X) is zero, for every n ≥ 8.\n\nIt is evident that the determinant of the monoid LC1 is non-zero. The Cayley table of LC2 is as follows:\n\n![](<2503.06329_pg24_images/imageFile7.png>)\n\na1 a2 1\n\n![](<2503.06329_pg24_images/imageFile8.png>)\n\n- a1 a1 ⋅ a1\n\n![](<2503.06329_pg24_images/imageFile9.png>)\n\n- a2 ⋅ a2 a2 1 a1 a2 1\n\n\n![](<2503.06329_pg24_images/imageFile10.png>)\n\n![](<2503.06329_pg24_images/imageFile11.png>)\n\nwhich indicates that the determinant is non-zero. Now, suppose that 3 ≤ n ≤ 7. Let e be an idempotent of LCn and let W1W2⋯Wme denote the canonical\n\nform of e over A. As n ≤ 7 and there is a two-letter distance between every neighboring segments of W, we have me ≤ 2.\n\nBy Lemma 4.1, since n ≤ 7, we have\n\nl1 = Wi Wi = 1, and d(Wi−1,Wi)≥ 3  ≤ 1. Thus, it follows that 1 ≤ L ̃e ≤ 2.\n\nThen, we have the following cases: L ̃e = 1 and me = 1: In this case, e is an idempotent with a single segment of length greater\n\nthan one. Thus, the submatrix L̃e × R̃e contains only a single entity, which is e, and its determinant is non-zero.\n\nL ̃e = 1 and me = 2:\n\n",
